     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-11-03 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Google Disables Search in Schools Rejecting AI Features</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Google</span> <span>#AI</span> <span>#Education</span> <span>#Schools</span></div>
       <p>Google is entirely turning off its Search service in K-12 schools that opt out of its artificial intelligence (AI) features, the company informed The New York Times. This decision was also part of a broader education-related update issued by Google on Tuesday, stating that access to Gemini, Gemini Notebook, Gemini in Classroom, and Google Search &quot;will be entirely off&quot; for K-12 student accounts unless administrators explicitly enable them.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-03/google-disables-search-in-schools-rejecting-ai-features.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>School administrators will not be given the flexibility to disable Gemini-powered features independently without also turning off Search. Chris Phillips, Google’s vice president of education, explained to the NYT that &quot;Search and AI are deeply integrated.&quot; He added that schools choosing to disable Google's AI features &quot;will not have access to Search, and they’ll see a message from their school that indicates it’s not available.&quot;</p><p>This announcement comes months after Google initially activated its Gemini AI assistant by default within the Google Classroom app, a platform utilized by over 150 million teachers and students globally. This prior move had intensified skepticism surrounding Google’s introduction of AI in educational settings, particularly amidst reports linking AI use to declining test scores and other impacts on students' learning capabilities. Lawmakers had also pressed Google last month regarding the &quot;potential harms&quot; of deploying its AI products in schools. While Google is no longer enabling AI features by default, this choice now carries the consequence of losing Search functionality.</p><p>Google is also working to mitigate concerns about students using its AI features to find &quot;shortcuts&quot; rather than engaging with problem-solving processes. To address this, the company is embedding its Guided Learning feature, designed to help students work through problems, directly into its AI Overviews within Search. Google plans to commence testing this new feature in select US schools during the current month.</p><p>What to watch: The ongoing debate over AI's role in education and how schools navigate these integrated technology choices.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report accurately covers all key points from the source, including the forced integration of Search and AI, the specific reasons for the change, and the new guided learning feature.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1025411/google-turn-off-search-ai-features" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-11-03/google-disables-search-in-schools-rejecting-ai-features" data-title="Google Disables Search in Schools Rejecting AI Features"><b>Share:</b>
