     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-11-03 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Indian Americans Seek Expanded Role in 2026 US Midterms</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#World News</span> <span>#US Politics</span> <span>#Midterm Elections</span> <span>#Indian Americans</span></div>
       <p>Indian Americans are seeking a more prominent role in American politics, with numerous candidates from the community contesting key positions in the November 3 US midterm elections. These races encompass a wide range of offices, from the governorship of Ohio and seats in the US Congress to state legislatures and local bodies. Candidates, representing both the Republican and Democratic parties as well as independent platforms, are actively working to strengthen the community’s political representation nationwide. According to news agency PTI, Indian Americans are estimated to number 5.2 million in the US, and these elections present a significant opportunity for them to expand their presence in public office.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-03/indian-americans-seek-expanded-role-in-2026-us-midterms.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>A closely watched race involves Republican Vivek Ramaswamy, who is challenging Democrat Amy Acton for the Ohio governor’s seat. Born in Cincinnati, Ohio, to parents who immigrated from India, Ramaswamy first entered the national political spotlight during his 2024 campaign for the Republican presidential nomination. After withdrawing from that race in January 2024 and endorsing Donald Trump, he was selected alongside Elon Musk to co-lead the Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE), though he later left to pursue his political ambitions. Ramaswamy secured the Republican nomination in May 2026, following the launch of his gubernatorial campaign in February 2025. PTI reports that if elected, he would become the third governor of Indian heritage in the US, after Bobby Jindal and Nikki Haley, and potentially the first Hindu governor of an American state. Elsewhere, Maryland Lieutenant Governor Aruna Miller, born in Hyderabad, is seeking re-election, holding one of the highest elected executive positions occupied by an Indian-origin politician in the country.</p><p>Several incumbent Indian-American lawmakers are also seeking another term in the US House of Representatives. These include Democratic Congressmen Ro Khanna from California’s 17th Congressional District, Ami Bera, and Suhas Subramanyam, alongside Democratic Congresswoman Pramila Jayapal from Washington’s 7th Congressional District. PTI identifies Bera and Subramanyam among four Indian-origin Democratic lawmakers expected to return to the House. Ro Khanna faces a challenge from Republican Ritesh Tandon, an Indian-American entrepreneur, making their contest an example of intra-community political competition. Similarly, Pramila Jayapal is contested by Republican Nirav Sheth, a Gujarat-born former US Marine. The community's political reach extends beyond federal and state legislatures, with Nithya Raman in the race to become the mayor of Los Angeles, the second-largest city in the US, marking a significant bid for a city-level leadership position. Additionally, Pia Dandiya, a former school principal, is seeking election to the US House of Representatives from Florida, engaged in a closely contested race against Republican Casey Aksar, PTI reported.</p><p>What to watch: The November 3 midterm elections will be a key indicator of the expanding political influence and representation of Indian Americans across various levels of US government.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft comprehensively covers the candidates and specific details provided in the source material. It is well-structured and accurate to the provided text.</p>
