     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-03 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Indian Law Protects Deceased Composers' Moral Rights</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#finance</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Under Section 57 of India's Copyright Act, heirs of deceased music composers retain moral rights even after copyright ownership is transferred to labels. These include the right of attribution, ensuring the composer is credited, and the right of integrity, which allows legal representatives to challenge distortions that damage the author's reputation.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-03/indian-law-protects-deceased-composers-moral-rights.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The 2012 Copyright Amendment Act empowered legal heirs to enforce these rights and seek damages for prejudicial modifications. While labels hold the commercial right to create adaptations like remixes, they cannot remove a composer's name or alter works in ways that harm the author's honor.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately synthesizes the legal explanation regarding Section 57 of the Copyright Act and the scope of moral rights for heirs.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/copyright-act-section-57-moral-rights-music-composer-copyright-assignment-music-labels-remix-rights-right-of-attribution-11793683479837.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-11-03/indian-law-protects-deceased-composers-moral-rights" data-title="Indian Law Protects Deceased Composers' Moral Rights"><b>Share:</b>
