     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · BBC Science · 2026-11-03 · editor 8/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Jersey Faces 'Biodiversity Crisis' Amid Government Environmental Focus Concerns</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Environment</span> <span>#Biodiversity</span> <span>#Jersey</span></div>
       <p>Jersey is grappling with a &quot;biodiversity crisis,&quot; a concern highlighted during a scrutiny panel hearing, amid wider apprehension that the government is not adequately prioritizing the environment. Willie Peggie, government Director of Natural Environment, stated that the Environment Department requires sufficient time and resources to produce a comprehensive state of nature report.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-03/jersey-faces-biodiversity-crisis-amid-government-environment.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Piers Sangan, who leads Sangan Island Conversation, described the biodiversity crisis as encompassing an increase in invasive species, a reduction in native species, and a decline in the overall ecosystem. Sangan emphasized the government's responsibility to prevent the environment from being overlooked, calling it a &quot;fundamental part of our island.&quot; He noted that Jersey's biodiversity strategy has been &quot;put on the shelf&quot; since the early 2000s, leading to the loss of animals like stoats and a &quot;woeful lack of investment&quot; in ecology.</p><p>Environment Minister Senator Mary Le Hegarat acknowledged the need for proper countryside management due to rising invasive species. She affirmed that policies are being developed to further protect the environment, stating that ministers will &quot;do the work and set out the policies and strategies.&quot; Government officials anticipate releasing a state of nature report in 2027, which will serve as the basis for a new biodiversity strategy, similar to reports in the UK, Guernsey, and Alderney.</p><p>Concerns about biodiversity are intertwined with questions regarding the government's commitment to environmental spending. Deputy Hilary Jeune, chair of the environment scrutiny panel, pointed out that the ministers' draft Common Strategic Policy initially failed to account for the natural environment, necessitating an amendment. Professor Liz Bentley of the Jersey Climate Council suggested Jersey needs to allocate £10 million per year to achieve net zero by 2050, significantly more than the £4 million currently in the Climate Emergency Fund. Senator Le Hegarat, however, stressed the need for &quot;caution&quot; regarding spending, though she views investing in the fund as a positive step.</p><p>Joshua and Benjamin Voak, working for the Jersey Grazing Project, use cows and sheep to enhance and restore habitats. Joshua Voak expressed concern that the environment received little discussion in the summer's election, despite the numerous economic advantages of a healthy environment. Benjamin Voak highlighted biodiversity's crucial role for the island's air and water quality and the preservation of habitats. The ongoing crisis underscores the interconnectedness of ecological health and the island's future well-being.</p><p>What to watch: The release of Jersey's state of nature report in 2027 and the subsequent development of a new biodiversity strategy.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively covers the main points of the biodiversity crisis in Jersey. It is well-structured and uses the provided source material accurately.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8wy94ewzw03o?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Science</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
