     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · India Today · 2026-11-03 · editor 8/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>National Political Debates Flare Over SIR, Foreign Funding; US Midterm Elections Analyzed</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#India politics</span> <span>#Parliament</span> <span>#US elections</span> <span>#foreign funding</span></div>
       <p>The Union Home Minister has challenged the opposition to a debate on the Special Intensive Revision (SIR) in Parliament, according to India Today. Emphasizing the central government's readiness for discussion on any issue, the minister offered to convene a special parliamentary session to discuss the SIR, provided Parliament is allowed to function. This statement reiterated an earlier dare following an opposition call for a debate with the Prime Minister. Criticizing the opposition for running a &quot;mock parliament,&quot; the minister stated that merely carrying the Constitution does not equate to genuine respect or support for constitutional values.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-03/national-political-debates-flare-over-sir-foreign-funding-us.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Separately, a political confrontation has escalated over allegations regarding foreign funding and foreign influence, following statements on an alleged external plot. A challenge has been issued to Prime Minister Narendra Modi to engage in an open public debate to address questions over who maintains links with foreign entities and the nature of those associations. The proposed debate suggests convening before the media and the country's youth, with Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh chief Mohan Bhagwat suggested as a potential arbiter or judge. Further issues highlighted for public scrutiny include financial structures, procedural transparency surrounding a US deal, the Adani case closure, and references to Jeffrey Epstein, while pointing out the lack of defamation litigation on the foreign connection dispute.</p><p>A panel of journalists analyzed the visual and operational differences between Indian and American voting systems during the United States midterm elections. The discussion highlighted why American polling locations lack the long queues typically seen in India, citing widespread early voting and mail-in ballots, with over 54 million votes cast prior to election day. From Washington, DC, ground observations pointed to early voter demographics, particularly suburban women who traditionally influence outcomes for Democrats.</p><p>The panel further assessed what the midterm results could mean for India and the Donald Trump administration, noting bilateral trade targets of 500 billion dollars over five years and strategic defense partnerships. Analysts also discussed potential political shifts, impeachment hurdles in the Senate, and the unprecedented influence of corporate funding and major campaign contributors during the election cycle. Speaking to India Today, Alec Tyson, Senior Vice President and lead pollster at Ipsos US Public Affairs, analyzed opinion poll projections for the US midterm elections. Tyson observed that polling indicates clear signals favoring the Democratic Party, driven by shifts in President Donald Trump's approval ratings.</p><p>Explaining voter sentiment, Tyson noted economic frustration centered on inflation and daily living costs, stating, &quot;It's the economy, but it's very personal. It's prices, it's affordability, it's a sense that it's hard to get ahead in this country today.&quot; He added that while incumbents traditionally lose House seats, Democratic momentum is unusually visible in historically Republican Senate battlegrounds like Texas and Kansas. As voting continues in the US midterm elections with over 54 million early ballots already cast, multiple national polling projections show Democrats leading over Republicans. Analysts discuss voter sentiment, noting that the preference reflects negative voting against Republicans rather than positive endorsement of Democrats. Economic concerns, inflation, skyrocketing fuel prices, and prolonged foreign military engagements have emerged as key factors influencing the electorate. While historical precedents show White House incumbents often struggle in midterms, discussions highlight how the high-stakes outcome could shape future domestic and international policies.</p><p>What to watch: Developments in the proposed Parliament session, progression of the foreign funding debate, and the final results of the US midterm elections.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Captures the political debate and US midterm polling analysis well, though slightly broad in summary phrasing.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.indiatoday.in/india/video/special-parliament-session-challenge-sir-debate-and-clash-over-house-disruptions-ytvd-3031626-2026-11-03?utm_source=rss" rel="noopener">India Today</a></li></ul>
