       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · BBC Business · 2026-11-03 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study Finds Up to 22% of Online Job Postings Are 'Ghost Jobs'</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#business</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A recent study across the US, UK, and Germany reveals that up to 22% of online job advertisements are posted with no actual intent to hire. These &quot;ghost jobs&quot; have become a significant concern for job seekers, with the term recently becoming a shortlist candidate for the Collins dictionary word of the year.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-03/study-finds-up-to-22-of-online-job-postings-are-ghost-jobs.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>BBC Business reporter Megan Lawton noted that some governments are currently exploring bans on the practice. Career coach Patricia from Nyche careers and job seeker Sundus provided further insights into the phenomenon during a recent episode of the BBC's What in the World podcast.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate brief on ghost jobs, incorporating study stats and podcast guests effectively.</p>
