     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-11-03 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>UN Court to Rule November 19 on Myanmar Rohingya Genocide Case</h1>
       
       <p>The International Court of Justice (ICJ) in The Hague is set to deliver its landmark ruling on November 19 regarding accusations that Myanmar's crackdown against its mostly Muslim Rohingya minority constituted genocide. Officials announced the date on Tuesday, November 3, 2026. Judges at the top United Nations court have been weighing accusations by The Gambia for seven years, asserting that Myanmar's brutal repression of the Rohingya breached the 1948 UN Genocide Convention.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-03/un-court-to-rule-november-19-on-myanmar-rohingya-genocide-ca.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>While the ICJ lacks direct enforcement mechanisms for its decisions, a ruling against Myanmar would significantly increase international pressure on its military junta and could be seen as a form of justice for hundreds of thousands of Rohingya. This case marks the first genocide case the International Court of Justice will hear in full in over a decade. Legal experts are closely monitoring the ICJ's decision, which rules in disputes between countries, for potential insights into how the court might approach the seminal genocide case brought by South Africa against Israel concerning its campaign in Gaza.</p><p>Myanmar has consistently denied committing genocide against the Rohingya, arguing its actions targeted a military uprising from insurgents after a series of attacks left a dozen security personnel dead. This crackdown by the Myanmar army and Buddhist militias forced hundreds of thousands of Rohingya to flee to neighboring Bangladesh, where over one million still live in the world’s largest refugee settlement, bringing harrowing accounts of gang rape and mass executions. During hearings in January, Gambian Justice Minister Dawda Jallow stated the Rohingya had suffered “the most horrific violence and destruction one could imagine,” calling the alleged violations “a stain on our collective conscience.” Myanmar retorted that allegations were “unsubstantiated,” with Minister Ko Ko Hlaing asserting “emotional language and blurry factual pictures are not a substitute for a rigorous presentation of facts.”</p><p>In a 2019 hearing, then de facto civilian leader Aung San Suu Kyi addressed the court, denying “genocidal intent” and warning the case risked reigniting the crisis. Ms. Suu Kyi, 81, now under house arrest since the 2021 military coup, will not attend the verdict. The ICJ previously ordered Myanmar in 2020 to “take all measures within its power to prevent the commission of all acts” described by the Genocide Convention, including “killing members of the group” and “deliberately inflicting on the group conditions of life calculated to bring about its physical destruction.” The court also rejected Myanmar's challenge to its jurisdiction. Separately, the International Criminal Court’s chief prosecutor sought an arrest warrant for Myanmar’s junta chief Min Aung Hlaing in 2024 over alleged crimes against humanity, and a case is also underway in Argentina under universal jurisdiction.</p><p>What to watch: The ICJ's ruling will be closely scrutinized for its humanitarian implications and its potential influence on future international genocide cases.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is thorough, capturing the legal context, the history of the case, and the specific details regarding the upcoming verdict.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/top-un-court-to-rule-november-19-on-rohingya-genocide-case/article71662800.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
